SPEC = {
 "file":"cover_lovable_data_scientist",
 "label":"Lovable Data Scientist",
 "p1":"I am writing to apply for the Data Scientist role at Lovable. I am completing an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a data analyst at TikTok Shop UK. The posting describes building an analytics function from the ground up through metrics frameworks, behavioural models and hypothesis testing, and that is the combination of commercial judgement and statistical rigour I have spent the past two years building.",
 "p2":"What attracts me to Lovable is the specific claim the product makes: that the constraint on software has been capital, technical fluency and time, and that removing it lets people build for problems they actually have. Sixty million projects since November 2024 is the kind of growth where the interesting questions are behavioural rather than technical, and where getting the metrics wrong early compounds. Joining a small, talent-dense team to build that muscle rather than inherit it is precisely the job I want.",
 "p3":"At TikTok Shop UK I do this work daily. I query merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python to run funnel and cohort analyses that explain why commercial performance shifted, build the KPI frameworks operations teams use to set targets, and maintain the dashboards that let them answer their own questions instead of queueing for an analyst. The most useful contribution I make is usually pushing back when a metric is measuring the wrong thing, which is a harder conversation early in a function's life and a much cheaper one.",
 "p4":"On experimentation, at DS Digital Technology I designed and ran blinded paired A/B tests measuring whether a medically reviewed knowledge graph improved answer quality, owning the process from hypothesis through to final recommendation. I also ran a repeated-measures study across 12 audience segments and 15 marketing touchpoints using Friedman tests, permutation tests and post-hoc comparisons, and turned it into per-segment playbooks the client acted on. Designing a test that can actually distinguish a real effect from noise is the part I enjoy most.",
 "p5":"The posting mentions leveraging AI tools and automation to scale analysis, which is how I already work. At DS Digital I built a Python pipeline connecting six large language models with retrieval augmentation that produced and scored more than 2,400 responses through an LLM-as-judge framework, because evaluating them by hand was not feasible. I use Claude Code daily for debugging and code review, and I treat its output as a draft to be checked rather than an answer, which I think is the only way to get leverage from it without quietly importing errors.",
 "p6":"My MSc dissertation is where the statistical discipline comes from. I built an end-to-end pipeline on longitudinal data covering 458,840 participants, engineered behavioural features across multiple time points, and estimated individual-level counterfactual outcomes under behaviour change. It also taught me to report null results plainly: several of the effects I tested were not there, and saying so was more useful than finding a way to make them appear.",
 "p7":"I would bring strong SQL and Python, experimental design that stands up to scrutiny, and experience turning analysis into narratives that non-technical teams act on. I should be straightforward that I am early career, so I have not yet owned an analytics function end to end, and building one from a near-blank canvas is the reason I am applying rather than something I would need easing into. I hold the right to work in the UK without sponsorship until January 2029. Thank you for considering my application; I would welcome the opportunity to discuss how I could help Lovable understand what actually drives growth and retention.",
}
